    {
      "predicate": "iadas:authorConclusion",
      "object": "Correlation obtained between anxiety (both trait and state) and the EAT-40 was positively and statistically significant. In the multiple regression analysis that was performed with SCOFF scores as a dependent variable, the most predictive variable was socially prescribed perfectionism. It showed negative correlation with EAT-40 and self esteem. Self esteem is probably a protective factor to develop an eating disorder despite perfectionism, which is the most predictive variable as the multiple linear regression showed, and this variable was high in athletes."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:limites",
      "object": "Small sample size. No precise clinical diagnostic data were available to validate ED screening in the current study."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:perspectives",
      "object": "In the future, the use of other kinds of measurements, less subjected to the desirability bias, would help to discriminate whether the improvements in self-esteem and eating disorders in elite gymnasts are due to this bias or to preventing psychological treatments and trainings by coaches.\n In addition, it is important to further explore the impact of other psychological factors on eating disordered behaviours such as body image, mood, depression or motivation, among others; due to the fact that they imply multifactorial aetiology of disorders."
    },
